WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.217
NARRATOR: Captions are not decorative
subtitles added at the end.

2
00:00:03.217 --> 00:00:08.230
NARRATOR: They carry the information contained
in the audio: spoken words, speaker identity

3
00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:10.543
when it matters, and meaningful sounds.

4
00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:14.594
NARRATOR: A viewer should be able to follow
the instructional message without relying on

5
00:00:14.594 --> 00:00:15.462
hearing the soundtrack.

6
00:00:15.578 --> 00:00:20.423
NARRATOR: Automatic speech recognition can
create a useful first draft, but technical

7
00:00:20.423 --> 00:00:23.653
terms, names, punctuation, and timing often
need correction.

8
00:00:23.653 --> 00:00:28.662
NARRATOR: Review captions against the final
audio, keep cues synchronized, and break lines

9
00:00:28.662 --> 00:00:30.203
at readable phrase boundaries.

10
00:00:30.203 --> 00:00:34.914
NARRATOR: The reviewed caption file—not the
raw machine output—belongs with the release.

11
00:00:35.018 --> 00:00:39.502
NARRATOR: Accessibility works best as a
production requirement, not an emergency

12
00:00:39.502 --> 00:00:39.910
repair.

13
00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:45.042
NARRATOR: RIT recommends planning accessible
media while a course is designed and provides

14
00:00:45.042 --> 00:00:47.016
captioning options for Panopto content.

15
00:00:47.016 --> 00:00:52.431
NARRATOR: Build review time into the schedule,
keep a corrected transcript, and verify that

16
00:00:52.431 --> 00:00:56.299
visuals also communicate their essential
meaning through narration or description.
